⟦P0⟧Go Threat Model⟦P1⟧Overview⟦P2⟧This document defines the general threat model for the Go toolchain and standard⟦P3⟧library. In the absence of package documentation defining a threat model, the⟦P4⟧model described in this document should be assumed to apply to all packages in⟦P5⟧the standard library.⟦P6⟧Threat Model⟦P7⟧Build Safety⟦P8⟧Building Go code is assumed to be safe and should have no side-effects, such as⟦P9⟧unexpected execution.⟦P10⟧Runtime Execution⟦P11⟧We generally do not consider the execution of malicious code to be a relevant⟦P12⟧security issue. A user who is familiar with Go is assumed to understand what⟦P13⟧they are executing.⟦P14⟧Memory Safety⟦P15⟧It is assumed that in the absence of usages of the ⟦P16⟧ package, memory⟦P17⟧safety is guaranteed by the runtime.⟦P18⟧Trust Boundaries⟦P19⟧APIs which are reasonably expected to accept arbitrary user provided input are⟦P20⟧assumed to be hardened against panics and arbitrary resource consumption.⟦P21⟧Passing garbage to an ⟦P22⟧ resulting in unexpected output is not considered a⟦P23⟧security issue.⟦P24⟧Environment Control⟦P25⟧It is assumed that the local system is safe. Attacks which rely on the ⟦P26⟧ already⟦P27⟧being compromised are not considered relevant. For instance we do not consider⟦P28⟧attacker control over the filesystem, environment variables, such as ⟦P29⟧, or⟦P30⟧memory access or control to be part of our model.⟦P31⟧Packages With Their Own Models⟦P32⟧⟦P33⟧image⟦P34⟧⟦P35⟧
